---
import DocsNav from "../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { evidenceRows, metrics, repositoryUrl } from "../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Evidence | OrbitKV"
  description="H20 memory, admission, owning-control, and CUDA VMM evidence for OrbitKV."
  active="Evidence"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
    <h1>Each result keeps its boundary.</h1>
    <p>
      A result fixes source, GPU, workload, method, and excluded claims.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Evidence" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Current H20 results</h2>
      <div class="metric-grid compact">
        {metrics.map((item) => (
          <article><strong>{item.value}</strong><div><h3>{item.label}</h3><p>{item.detail}</p></div></article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Public gpt-oss-20b checkpoint.</strong>
        <p>
          Stock SGLang and OrbitKV loaded the same complete 13.761 GB MXFP4
          checkpoint with FA3 attention. Under the same 1.979 GiB KV budget,
          interval 32 increased Full capacity from 47,616 to 59,904 tokens. A
          balanced four-way ablation showed Stock32 reproduces that benefit:
          Stock32/Stock128 was 0.7828x, Policy32/Stock32 was 0.9969x, and
          Owner32/Policy32 was 1.0218x. The full OrbitKV owner path remained
          20.30% below Stock128 with identical output-token digests. At fixed
          Full capacity, OrbitKV allocated 288 MiB less KV with a 0.9992x
          median ratio. The contribution is automatic state-plan synthesis and
          proof-carrying ownership, not a proprietary interval or faster
          attention kernel.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/h20-gpt-oss-20b-real-validation-20260817.md`}>
          Real-checkpoint validation
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Physical policy compiler.</strong>
        <p>
          OrbitKV evaluated intervals 16, 32, 64, and 128 against the recorded
          KV budget, eight-request admission target, and four-call reclamation
          budget. It rejected 16 for excess reclaim calls, rejected 128 for a
          second admission wave, and selected 32. Four fresh SGLang processes
          matched every predicted Full and SWA pool capacity exactly. A real
          Owner run consumed the complete artifact and passed its cache, page,
          execution-mode, and pool-capacity contract.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/h20-gpt-oss-20b-real-20260817/candidate-validation.json`}>
          Candidate validation
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Declarative Retention IR.</strong>
        <p>
          The relation <code>query_position - key_position &lt; 1024</code>
          inferred a proven delta upper bound of 1023, a 1,024-token lifetime,
          and a 65-cell periodic layout at page size 16. Legacy Full/SWA JSON
          and the Retention IR emitted byte-identical artifacts, and the
          declarative plan completed a real H20 SGLang owner smoke.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/retention-ir-20260817/analysis.json`}>
          Retention analysis
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Lifetime normalization: Sink + Sliding.</strong>
        <p>
          One relation, <code>key &lt; 4 OR query - key &lt; 8</code>,
          compiled into a one-cell pinned sink and a three-cell periodic local
          region. Host exhaustive tests compare generated continuation sets
          with the source relation across page sizes, windows, and boundaries,
          while checking that live local blocks never collide. This is compiler
          and reference-runtime evidence, not an H20 or SGLang speedup result.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/sink-sliding-20260817/summary.json`}>
          Sink + Sliding proof summary
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Address synthesis: Chunked Local.</strong>
        <p>
          The relation <code>floor(q / 16) == floor(k / 16)</code>
          compiled into a four-cell resettable arena at page size four. Every
          block in an epoch receives the same chunk-end retirement boundary.
          Simulator and Manager tests hold old epoch cells until reader
          completion and backend commit. This is host-only compiler/runtime
          evidence, not an SGLang performance result.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/chunked-local-20260817/summary.json`}>
          Chunked Local proof summary
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Evidence ledger</h2>
      <table class="evidence-table-native">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Result</th><th>Value</th><th>Contract</th><th>Boundary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {evidenceRows.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{item.result}</td>
              <td><code>{item.value}</code></td>
              <td>{item.contract}</td>
              <td>{item.boundary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Owning control plane.</strong>
        <p>
          The in-process FFI reduced release-build <code>plan + commit</code>
          median latency from 31.03 µs to 7.29 µs. Six alternating-order H20
          pairs produced a median FFI/sidecar ratio of 1.0030x with identical
          capacity and output digest. The 4.26x transport result is not a
          serving speedup claim.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/owner-ffi-20260817/summary.json`}>
          Owner FFI summary
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>CUDA VMM physical primitive.</strong>
        <p>
          Sixty-four fresh physical backings were mapped at one stable virtual
          address. Every pattern passed, and GPU memory returned to the pre-test
          value. The pinned SGLang revision already has monotonic post-capture
          VMM backing; OrbitKV adds generation-aware reclaim/remap receipts.
          A separate closed-loop test passed 64 CUDA Events, 64 data checks,
          63 stale-generation rejections, and 65 manager receipt commits. This
          is not yet SGLang KV tensor storage.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/h20-cuda-vmm-20260817/smoke.json`}>
          VMM smoke record
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Current exclusions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No released-checkpoint model-quality improvement claim.</li>
        <li>No radix/prefix-cache, speculative, overlap, or CUDA Graph qualification.</li>
        <li>No claim that VMM already backs SGLang KV tensors.</li>
        <li>No multi-GPU multicast or fabric-memory claim.</li>
        <li>One H20 does not qualify other NVIDIA architectures.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The complete result index and source hashes are in{" "}
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/tree/main/results`}>results/</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
